WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:13.667 --> 00:00:15.268
I'm not sure what you might think

00:00:15.292 --> 00:00:17.976
when you think about
the job of a police officer.

00:00:18.000 --> 00:00:21.934
Recent events have sparked lots of debate
over the role of law enforcement

00:00:21.958 --> 00:00:23.309
in our society

00:00:23.333 --> 00:00:24.976
and if it should change.

00:00:25.000 --> 00:00:28.375
And that's a big, important conversation
that we all need have.

00:00:29.625 --> 00:00:32.018
But today I'd like to talk about something

00:00:32.042 --> 00:00:34.184
that's at the core
to my day-to-day work --

00:00:34.208 --> 00:00:38.018
something not often discussed
when talking about police work,

00:00:38.042 --> 00:00:41.976
and that's dealing
with trauma, hurt and loss.

00:00:42.000 --> 00:00:43.809
What's it like to tell someone --

00:00:43.833 --> 00:00:45.143
someone that they know,

00:00:45.167 --> 00:00:48.768
someone that they love died suddenly?

00:00:48.792 --> 00:00:52.018
Many of you might think
this is done by hospitals or doctors.

00:00:52.042 --> 00:00:54.684
If you die there, well, it usually is.

00:00:54.708 --> 00:00:57.518
If you die outside the hospital,

00:00:57.542 --> 00:01:01.976
it's more often than not the police
who notify that nearest loved one.

00:01:02.000 --> 00:01:06.059
Doing that type of work
has taught me powerful lessons

00:01:06.083 --> 00:01:10.500
on approaching highly charged situations
in all areas of my life.

00:01:11.500 --> 00:01:15.101
My passion to connect
started about 10 years ago.

00:01:15.125 --> 00:01:18.125
I responded to a death call
that changed me.

00:01:19.042 --> 00:01:21.518
A woman -- let's call her Vicky.

00:01:21.542 --> 00:01:24.143
Vicky called because her husband
had suddenly collapsed

00:01:24.167 --> 00:01:26.059
in the hallway of their home.

00:01:26.083 --> 00:01:28.768
The first responders and I
tried everything.

00:01:28.792 --> 00:01:30.542
We gave it our best effort,

00:01:31.875 --> 00:01:33.559
but he died.

00:01:33.583 --> 00:01:35.226
In complete devastation,

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.268
Vicky fell to the floor.

00:01:37.292 --> 00:01:42.018
Instantly, I could feel us
strapping on that emotional armor,

00:01:42.042 --> 00:01:45.309
going right to work
on policies and procedures.

00:01:45.333 --> 00:01:48.726
I began peppering her with questions
like detailed medical history

00:01:48.750 --> 00:01:50.542
and funeral home arrangements.

00:01:51.667 --> 00:01:55.750
Questions that she couldn't possibly
have been prepared to answer.

00:01:56.583 --> 00:01:58.018
In an empathetic gesture,

00:01:58.042 --> 00:02:01.351
I reached down and I put
my hand on her shoulder.

00:02:01.375 --> 00:02:03.333
She flinched and pulled away.

00:02:04.125 --> 00:02:07.476
Suddenly, her neighbor came running in

00:02:07.500 --> 00:02:09.042
and instantly hugged her.

00:02:10.167 --> 00:02:12.226
Vicky pushed her away too.

00:02:12.250 --> 00:02:14.268
The neighbor seemed stunned,

00:02:14.292 --> 00:02:15.976
a little put off,

00:02:16.000 --> 00:02:17.292
and she walked back out.

00:02:18.125 --> 00:02:20.559
Then, to make matters worse,

00:02:20.583 --> 00:02:22.476
the medical examiner's office,

00:02:22.500 --> 00:02:25.893
carrying the body bag holding her husband,

00:02:25.917 --> 00:02:29.309
dropped it down a flight of stairs,

00:02:29.333 --> 00:02:31.708
crashing into a decorative end table.

00:02:33.250 --> 00:02:36.393
I will never forget the sound of her voice

00:02:36.417 --> 00:02:38.042
when she looked at me and said,

00:02:39.250 --> 00:02:40.708
"I wish I never called."

00:02:41.917 --> 00:02:43.250
I felt awful.

00:02:44.792 --> 00:02:48.268
Being confronted with death
can be difficult for everyone.

00:02:48.292 --> 00:02:51.833
Often we rely solely
on our instincts to help guide us.

00:02:52.458 --> 00:02:53.768
In law enforcement,

00:02:53.792 --> 00:02:55.851
we tend to put up an emotional shield,

00:02:55.875 --> 00:02:57.601
a barrier to emotions.

00:02:57.625 --> 00:03:00.184
That way we can focus
on policies and procedures

00:03:00.208 --> 00:03:01.458
to guide us.

00:03:02.125 --> 00:03:05.417
This is why we can sometimes
come across as robotic.

00:03:06.208 --> 00:03:08.726
I've discovered
that in the civilian world,

00:03:08.750 --> 00:03:12.018
you're often driven
by that instinct to fix it,

00:03:12.042 --> 00:03:15.625
usually done with well-intended
comments or physical touch.

00:03:16.500 --> 00:03:18.958
Sometimes that may be that right answer.

00:03:20.292 --> 00:03:22.000
Other times, not.

00:03:23.958 --> 00:03:27.518
Had I slowed down and just taken a breath,

00:03:27.542 --> 00:03:31.018
I would have been better able
to connect to the humanity of that moment.

00:03:31.042 --> 00:03:35.684
I could have avoided that policy
and procedure, check-the-box mentality.

00:03:35.708 --> 00:03:38.309
Her neighbor, had she slowed down,

00:03:38.333 --> 00:03:39.934
just taken a breath,

00:03:39.958 --> 00:03:43.059
she may have been able to see
that in that moment,

00:03:43.083 --> 00:03:46.601
Vicky just wasn't prepared for touch.

00:03:46.625 --> 00:03:48.809
Our hearts may have been
in the right place,

00:03:48.833 --> 00:03:53.083
but we made it about us
instead of focusing on her.

00:03:54.000 --> 00:03:55.476
In complete contrast,

00:03:55.500 --> 00:03:56.976
more recently,

00:03:57.000 --> 00:03:59.542
I met a woman -- let's call her Monica.

00:04:00.417 --> 00:04:02.143
I was tasked to tell Monica

00:04:02.167 --> 00:04:05.250
that her husband had tragically
taken his own life.

00:04:06.417 --> 00:04:10.268
She fell to the floor crying so hard
she could barely breathe.

00:04:10.292 --> 00:04:13.559
The gravity of that moment was so strong,

00:04:13.583 --> 00:04:17.059
but I knew I needed to resist
that urge to move in

00:04:17.083 --> 00:04:18.893
and to comfort her.

00:04:18.917 --> 00:04:20.434
That sounds crazy, right?

00:04:20.458 --> 00:04:22.101
Honestly, it's excruciating.

00:04:22.125 --> 00:04:25.333
In your mind and in your heart,
you just want to hug this person.

00:04:26.042 --> 00:04:27.333
But I stopped myself.

00:04:28.208 --> 00:04:30.684
Having been around trauma
for over 20 years,

00:04:30.708 --> 00:04:32.184
I will tell you

00:04:32.208 --> 00:04:35.184
not everybody is comfortable
with human touch.

00:04:35.208 --> 00:04:36.934
There are people all over the world

00:04:36.958 --> 00:04:40.018
suffering from physical
or psychological trauma

00:04:40.042 --> 00:04:41.583
you may know nothing about.

00:04:42.542 --> 00:04:46.184
Who knows what they're thinking
or feeling in those moments.

00:04:46.208 --> 00:04:47.518
If I move in,

00:04:47.542 --> 00:04:50.143
if I touch her like I did Vicky,

00:04:50.167 --> 00:04:55.125
I could unintentionally
revictimize her all over again.

00:04:56.333 --> 00:04:58.268
Think: respect space.

00:04:58.292 --> 00:05:00.518
Be guided by respect space.

00:05:00.542 --> 00:05:03.893
It's a simple concept with a huge impact.

00:05:03.917 --> 00:05:07.958
You can't step into that space
until you're invited.

00:05:09.208 --> 00:05:10.667
So I sat across from Monica,

00:05:11.708 --> 00:05:13.351
silent,

00:05:13.375 --> 00:05:14.684
eye level,

00:05:14.708 --> 00:05:17.018
just feeling that moment.

00:05:17.042 --> 00:05:19.893
My heart was pounding
so hard I could hear it.

00:05:19.917 --> 00:05:21.226
That lump in my throat?

00:05:21.250 --> 00:05:23.893
Ugh, I -- I could barely swallow.

00:05:23.917 --> 00:05:25.643
And you know what?

00:05:25.667 --> 00:05:26.917
That's OK.

00:05:28.292 --> 00:05:32.893
Emotions and vulnerability
can be so hard for some people.

00:05:32.917 --> 00:05:34.393
I understand that.

00:05:34.417 --> 00:05:37.434
But in human moments, people want human.

00:05:37.458 --> 00:05:41.559
They don't want a robotic police officer
or to be talking about paperwork.

00:05:41.583 --> 00:05:44.333
They just want another human
to connect to them.

00:05:46.083 --> 00:05:47.893
As we sat together,

00:05:47.917 --> 00:05:52.309
she asked me one question
over and over and over again.

00:05:52.333 --> 00:05:55.309
"What am I supposed to tell my kids?"

00:05:55.333 --> 00:05:58.726
One of the most important parts
of respecting space

00:05:58.750 --> 00:06:01.768
is not always having to have an answer.

00:06:01.792 --> 00:06:05.059
I could feel she didn't want me
to answer that question.

00:06:05.083 --> 00:06:07.726
She didn't want me to try to fix
that unfixable moment.

00:06:07.750 --> 00:06:11.393
She wanted me to connect
to the depth of that experience

00:06:11.417 --> 00:06:13.018
she was going through.

00:06:13.042 --> 00:06:14.851
Yes, I had a job to do.

00:06:14.875 --> 00:06:16.559
And when the time was right,

00:06:16.583 --> 00:06:19.226
I asked the questions
that needed to be answered,

00:06:19.250 --> 00:06:21.292
but I did it at her pace.

00:06:22.417 --> 00:06:26.976
Responding to death calls has taught me
so much about the human experience

00:06:27.000 --> 00:06:30.375
and the best ways to be there for somebody
when they need you the most.

00:06:31.333 --> 00:06:34.143
But it doesn't always have to be
when dealing with death.

00:06:34.167 --> 00:06:37.268
There's never a bad time
to build a connection.

00:06:37.292 --> 00:06:40.101
Hearing a private
revelation from a friend,

00:06:40.125 --> 00:06:42.518
you could be such a better listener.

00:06:42.542 --> 00:06:44.643
In an argument with a loved one,

00:06:44.667 --> 00:06:47.518
by just stepping back
and giving that respect space,

00:06:47.542 --> 00:06:50.726
you could better connect
to their side of an issue.

00:06:50.750 --> 00:06:53.851
You may never be asked
to tell a complete stranger

00:06:53.875 --> 00:06:55.601
that their loved one died,

00:06:55.625 --> 00:06:57.351
but we all have the opportunity

00:06:57.375 --> 00:07:00.809
to be the best, most connected
versions of ourselves,

00:07:00.833 --> 00:07:03.125
especially in times of need.

00:07:03.958 --> 00:07:06.434
That respect space
that you provide another

00:07:06.458 --> 00:07:09.917
can have a life-changing effect
on the people around you.

00:07:11.250 --> 00:07:12.500
Thank you.

